  "slug": "optimization-framework-skyrmionic-7fd4",
  "summary": "Recent preprints apply explicit optimization and predictive design frameworks to flying-focus, moiré metasurface, and skyrmionic polarimetry problems.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the design of structured optical fields through explicit optimization or predictive frameworks. A multilevel modulated-width zone plate design for flying-focus pulses suppresses higher-order foci by four orders of magnitude. A predictive framework for reflective moiré metasurfaces links beam multiplicity to the rotational symmetry and phase of Fourier components, targeting multibeam control. A multi-stage optimization framework for full-skyrmionic polarimetry introduces topological evaluating functions for accurate measurement of optical skyrmion states. The three preprints address different observables, namely spatiotemporal focusing, beam multiplicity, and polarization topology, so they share a design methodology rather than a physical system. What is new is the use of explicit design criteria, including modulation-width profiles, symmetry-phase relations, and topological evaluating functions, as the organizing principle for each design problem.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-18"
}
